Package: com.example.multifloat
Name: MultiFloat
Version: 0.13.0
Architecture: iphoneos-arm64e
Description: Multi-floating window / split-view tweak for iOS 17 (roothide / arm64e). 0.13.0 fixes the black floating window that 0.10.0 introduced and that 0.11.0 and 0.12.0 did not cure. The user's report for this round is short and decisive: switching apps still turns the floating window black. Archive 13 pins the cause down to one missing switch. In the Safari round the log shows deactivationReasons going from 0 to 32 while contentState, layers and hostedLayers are all still 2, so the picture is fine; our preventive write does fire; then in the very same second contentState drops to 0, and one tick later the layers drop to 0 and never come back. The presenter capability report, written while the scene was still being hosted, shows the other half of the story. The scene settings still say foreground = 1, but the target application's process state reads taskState: Suspended, visibility: Background. The app process itself was suspended. A suspended process cannot produce any drawing, which is why 81 consecutive full writes in the previous round never moved the layer count off zero. Comparing the effective write of 0.9.1 with the write of 0.10.0 and later leaves exactly one difference. 0.9.1 passes forceAll = YES whenever the layers are gone or contentState is not 2, and that forced doFg, so it called setForeground: YES on every single tick. From 0.10.0 onwards doFg has been gated by keepAliveForeground, which defaults to NO, so except for the one-off write before hosting, setForeground was never called again. That default came from a note in the 0.9.x code saying this device has no foreground and no setForeground, and the responder guard silently skips it. The note is wrong, and it is wrong in an instructive way: it asked the wrong object. It only tested respondsToSelector on FBScene, which indeed returns NO, and never asked the settings object. The existence matrix in this round asks the settings object and reports foreground, the getter, as missing while setForeground, the setter, is present. It is a write-only foreground switch, and it is precisely the switch 0.9.1 used to keep the app process from being suspended. 0.13.0 therefore does three things. First, keepAliveForeground defaults to true, and both the preventive and the reactive write paths now include setForeground: YES. Second, the drop criterion now includes FBScene.foreground, read through key-value coding, whenever it is readable; a value of 0 is the direct signal that the app is about to lose its foreground status. Third, and most importantly, the preventive write becomes a delta write. It reads every current value it can - idleModeEnabled, deactivationReasons, level and FBScene.foreground - and sends only the items that actually differ; if there is no delta at all it does not even call updateSettingsWithBlock, which means zero writes, zero recomputation and zero flash. Because setForeground may in principle be inert, the write is verified by reading FBScene.foreground back, and after three consecutive failures the tweak stops trying that switch so that it can never degenerate into one write per tick, which was the old 0.9.1 flash. Read the three write strategies this way: 0.9.1 prevented but wrote the full set every tick, so it never lost the picture but always flashed; 0.10.0 and 0.11.0 stopped preventing while still writing fully, so they did not flash but the layers were lost for good; 0.12.0 prevented but wrote only idle and deactivationReasons, so it did not flash but could not hold the scene either; 0.13.0 prevents and writes only the delta, including setForeground, so a single event causes a single write. The preventive tier also backs off now, doubling its interval up to two seconds after forty consecutive ticks, but it never gives up, because giving up is what produced the permanent black screen in 0.10.0. For A/B work, keepAliveForeground = false reproduces the 0.10.0 through 0.12.0 black screen, and keepAlivePreventHard = true restores the full 0.9.1 write including displayConfiguration and frame. The capability report gained two evidence lines: FBScene.foreground, and the real setter availability on the mutable settings object, probed inside the settings block rather than on the immutable scene settings, which is where every previous round went wrong.
